  [1mOne fork I didn't anticipate, and it's yours to call.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is loaded as a plain [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m
  ([38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m) — no module system. [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS ([38;5;153msrc/utils.js:5[39m). Those are two incompatible module worlds, and
  the seam has to live in one of them.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mimport { trackLogin } from './src/tracking.js'[39m. No build step, no globals, and
    it's the only option where the seam is unit-testable. Real cost: a [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m page [1mcan't be opened by [0m
    [1mdouble-clicking [38;5;153mindex.html[0m anymore — browsers block module loads over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so you'd need [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m or any static
    server. That's a change to how you run the thing.
  - [1mSecond [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag, global [38;5;153mwindow.trackLogin[0m — zero friction, still opens from [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Costs you a global and
    leaves the seam untestable without a DOM harness.
  - [1mCommonJS + a bundler[0m — matches [38;5;153msrc/[39m, but adds a build step to a repo that currently has none. Overkill for one
    function.

  [1mRecommendation: ES modules.[0m The [38;5;153mfile://[39m loss is the only real cost and it's small; in exchange the seam is testable,
  which matters because the whole point of it is that it'll be edited again when the endpoint lands.

  If you take ES modules, I'd also move [38;5;153mlogin[39m into [38;5;153msrc/auth.js[39m and leave [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as DOM wiring only. Not cosmetic: [38;5;153mapp.js
[39m  calls [38;5;153mdocument.getElementById[39m at top level ([38;5;153mapp.js:17[39m), so it throws the moment anything imports it — [38;5;153mlogin[39m cannot be
  tested at all while it lives there.

  [1mTooling.[0m This repo has none — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts, no dependencies, no test runner, no linter. Cheapest to set
  up now, before there's code to retrofit.
